WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
SPEAKER: So far, we've
covered many aspects

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.280
of using Cloud Operations
to improve the observability

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.797
and reliability of your services
running on Google Cloud.

00:00:08.797 --> 00:00:11.130
But how should you approach
setting up and using a suite

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.630
as your cloud footprint grows?

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.160
Today, we'll look
at some enterprise

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.860
best practices that you can
use to get the most value out

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.240
of Cloud Operations.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:22.150
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.350
Before we start, let's
take a step back and answer

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:29.490
this simple question.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.710
Why do we do all
of these things?

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.570
What are we trying
to achieve, overall,

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.150
when using a suite
like Cloud Operations?

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.860
Ultimately, we're after
three main objectives.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:41.790
The first one is to make sure
that our services are secure

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.400
and that we comply with
policies and regulations that

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.100
are specifically
relevant in our context.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.630
That means we need
to manage things

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.318
like data access
and an audit trail.

00:00:51.318 --> 00:00:52.860
The second one is
the objective we've

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.780
talked about in just
about every episode,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.490
keeping our users happy.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.040
That means we need to know
if user experience degrades,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.780
hopefully by using SLOs,
and what to do about it.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.870
The final goal is
managing the capacity

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.150
required to run our
services while ensuring

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.070
that we optimize for cost.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.290
With that said, we'll
tackle best practices

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:13.900
for setting up and using
Cloud Operations by focusing

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.890
on these three key areas.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.550
The first one, and the
one we'll focus on today,

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.830
will be best practices
for the enterprise.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:22.750
We'll cover setting up
a centralized logging

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.570
and monitoring environment,
ensuring an audit trail,

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.710
and configuring data
retention and access.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:32.290
The next episode will address
best practices for using

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.150
Cloud Monitoring specifically.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.560
We'll get into what to
monitor, how to do it,

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.120
how to manage alerts, and how to
optimize your monitoring costs.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.550
The final episode on this
topic will cover best practices

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:43.870
for Cloud Logging.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.700
We'll get into what
and how to log,

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.040
how to use the logs
your services generate,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.170
and how to optimize
your logging costs.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.550
This is going to be a lot,
but I think you're ready.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:54.600
Let's get into it.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.920
Let's start by talking about
enterprise best practices.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.830
The first recommendation
is to centralize

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.500
monitoring, auditing,
and observability data

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.850
in a single place.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.830
For services running
on Google Cloud,

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.250
we recommend using Cloud
Operations to do just that.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.440
Having this information
in a single repository

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.750
with easy cross-team
access greatly

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.000
improves practices like
architecture design,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.980
incident response, and
root cause analysis.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.910
For example, if I know the
historical reliability track

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:27.090
record of my dependencies, I can
better architect my own service

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:29.160
to meet my reliability
objectives.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.280
At the same time, I can do a
better job of incident response

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.350
if I can see whether
the services I depend on

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.607
are having an incident.

00:02:35.607 --> 00:02:37.440
It's really difficult
to do things like this

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.530
without having all this
information in a single place.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.540
The second recommendation
is to ensure

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.220
that a clear and
easily accessible

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:46.780
audit trail exists
for your services.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.060
This means using
cloud audit logs

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.170
to help answer questions
like who did what,

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.610
where, and when in your Google
Cloud projects, and managing

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:00.420
IAM controls to limit who has
access to view audit logs.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.430
We've covered using audit logs
in detail in another video.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.430
We'll link to it
in the show notes.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.890
As you make audit log
configuration changes,

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:10.470
use a test Google Cloud
project to validate them

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:14.410
before propagating to developer
and production projects.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.390
When considering how you
grant access to audit

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:18.880
logs across the
organization, we recommend

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.950
using a least privilege approach
to granting permissions.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.666
We'll link to specific examples
of that in the episode notes.

00:03:25.666 --> 00:03:28.810
Note that data access audit
logs are off by default,

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:30.250
except for BigQuery.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.770
When you enable new
Google Cloud Services,

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.720
evaluate whether or
not to enable data

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.270
access audit logs
for that new service.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.130
The final recommendation
for enterprises

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.380
is to thoughtfully manage
log retention and analysis.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:44.930
Consider the various
retention needs

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.720
for compliance requirements,
security and access analytics,

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.160
and event analytics.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.900
We'll link to
documentation explaining

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:54.560
specific considerations for each
scenario in the episode notes.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.380
The first way to do this
is by using log views.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.030
You can use log
views to control who

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.220
has access to logs
within your log buckets

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.470
based on your needs
and requirements.

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:05.570
Custom log views provides
you with a granular way

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:10.040
to control access to the
logs in your log buckets.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.350
You can also carefully
control log retention

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.750
by using project-level
or aggregated sinks

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.110
to export logs to BigQuery,
Cloud Storage, and Pub/Sub

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:21.870
for analytics, archival, or
consumption by other systems.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:23.630
Each of these do have
their own mechanisms

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:25.605
for controlling
retention and access,

00:04:25.605 --> 00:04:27.230
and you should
carefully consider those

00:04:27.230 --> 00:04:30.510
as you plan your
retention strategy.

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:31.930
Thanks for joining me today.

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:34.020
We covered best practices
for setting up and using

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.740
Cloud Operations
in the enterprise,

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:38.490
including centralizing data,
managing an audit trail,

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.475
and configuring data retention.

00:04:40.475 --> 00:04:41.850
I hope these
recommendations help

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.490
you keep your services
secure and your users happy.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.860
In our next episode, we'll
tackle best practices

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.270
for using Cloud Monitoring.

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:49.990
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.320
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.140
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.640
See you soon.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

